# iOS build numbers — never reuse an ASC build string

App Store Connect rejects reused `CFBundleVersion` values (`Redundant Binary Upload` / 90189).
Marketing version (`CFBundleShortVersionString` / `MARKETING_VERSION`) stays at `1.0` unless
product explicitly bumps it — that is **not** the number ASC complained about.

## What actually makes every Codemagic upload work

Root `codemagic.yaml` **Set build number** asks App Store Connect for the latest build
(TestFlight + App Store), then stamps **`latest + 1`**. That is the primary guarantee.

`MIN_IOS_BUILD_NUMBER` is only a **safety floor / fallback** if ASC lookup fails.

## Merge-to-main rule

**Every merge into `main` must still raise `MIN_IOS_BUILD_NUMBER` by 1** (watermark).

Prefer:

```bash
./scripts/bump-min-ios-build-number.sh
```

There is also `.github/workflows/bump-min-ios-on-main.yml` which auto-bumps after a push
to `main`. If that workflow is enabled, agents should still bump in the PR when practical
so the floor is correct even before the bot commit lands — but **do not double-bump** in
the same merge if the PR already raised the floor for that merge.

## Do not

- Skip the bump because the PR is “docs only”, “web only”, or unrelated to iOS
- Confuse marketing **`1.0`** with the build string (`CFBundleVersion`)
- Rely on `max(BUILD_NUMBER, FLOOR)` alone — that collides when FLOOR > BUILD_NUMBER and
  you rebuild without ASC advancing (the old failure mode)
- Stamp the same floor onto two Codemagic runs
